WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.238
Unit 2 is the failure modes, which
you learn before you enforce.

2
00:00:04.238 --> 00:00:08.444
A disabled or missing client is
blocked at the next authentication;

3
00:00:08.444 --> 00:00:11.825
existing sessions continue
until reauthentication.

4
00:00:11.825 --> 00:00:16.663
The response is to restrict disabling and
give the help desk a reinstall script.

5
00:00:16.663 --> 00:00:18.830
In a Global Secure Access outage,

6
00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:22.200
users can't reach the
resources the check protects.

7
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.331
Microsoft's break-glass scripts
switch these policies to report-only,

8
00:00:26.331 --> 00:00:30.462
and the guide rehearses them and
records them in the contingency plan.

9
00:00:30.462 --> 00:00:34.177
Turning signaling off while
policies are on blocks users,

10
00:00:34.177 --> 00:00:37.737
so the rollback order is
policies first, signaling last.

11
00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:40.724
The check isn't supported
for Private Access apps,

12
00:00:40.724 --> 00:00:43.712
which get the per-app
policies of Module 4 instead.

13
00:00:43.712 --> 00:00:48.449
And mobile devices need Defender
exclusions, outside this Windows pilot.

14
00:00:48.449 --> 00:00:52.512
One more piece: Universal
Continuous Access Evaluation.

15
00:00:52.512 --> 00:00:56.681
The Windows client one point
eight point two thirty-nine or

16
00:00:56.681 --> 00:01:00.763
later uses Universal C A E
tokens, revoked in near real time.

17
00:01:00.763 --> 00:01:05.029
Learn's recommendation is not to
disable C A E for these workloads,

18
00:01:05.029 --> 00:01:10.220
and the guide decides strict location
enforcement per policy, not tenant-wide.
